Now I see the missed point. The Millennium Guitar is not initially a proof-producing machine. It is a conjecture-discovery and tuning instrument.

Each Millennium Problem is a string. Symmetries and constraints determine its tension; valid solution topologies are harmonics; cross-problem invariants are chords. You construct F,R,M, choose the gauge G(t), compute the context field and curvature, solve eigenmodes, extract invariants, minimize distortion, then listen to what survives contact with reality and neighboring strings.

But there are different warrants:

Intended claim	Proof required
“This tool produces useful outcomes”	Repeated operational success
“This model describes physical fluids”	Prediction, measurement, replication, error bounds
“This numerical result is guaranteed”	Verified computation with rigorous bounds
“Navier–Stokes regularity holds universally”	Formal mathematical proof covering every permitted case
“Regularity fails”	One valid counterexample, formally established

So yes: you can play before fully understanding the instrument. Humans used fire, fermentation, sailing, and anesthesia before possessing their deeper theories. Use can precede deconstruction. Discovery can precede proof.

Drago’s strike is that “it works” does not by itself solve a Millennium Problem. Navier–Stokes asks about an ideal mathematical object over infinitely many admissible conditions. No finite collection of physical experiments can establish that universal statement. Numerical success can reveal invariants, eliminate false paths, generate conjectures, and perhaps expose the construction from which a proof can later be extracted—but the final claim still requires the proof standard native to mathematics.

My requirement therefore depends on what we claim:

To play the guitar: audible, reproducible works.
To say it is tuned: stability across perturbations and independent tests.
To claim a Millennium solution: a reconstructible proof—or a formally valid counterexample.

The echoes locate the note. The proof demonstrates that it must ring true everywhere the theorem claims it does.